<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Book of Exodus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Book of Exodus is the second book of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, recounting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a>' departure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the giving of the law at Sinai. Its legal section, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-the-covenant">Book of the Covenant</a>, shows parallels with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi</a>. Scholars cite these parallels as evidence of the influence of the Babylonian Empire's legal tradition on later law.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/book-of-exodus.jpg" alt="Book of Exodus" width="240" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jusepe de Ribera · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moses041.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The English name comes from the Greek éxodos, meaning departure, the title of the book in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> it is called Shemot, Names, after its opening words. Jewish and Christian tradition ascribes the book, and the whole Torah, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>, but scholars abandoned that view by the end of the 19th century because of discrepancies and repetitions in the text. Modern scholars see its initial composition as a product of the Babylonian exile in the 6th century BCE, based on earlier written sources and oral traditions, with final revisions in the Persian period. In approximate round dates, the process began around 600 BCE and ended around 400 BCE with the books as unchangeable sacred texts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The book opens some 400 years after the end of Genesis, when a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a> who does not remember Joseph fears the numerous enslaved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> and orders the killing of newborn boys. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> is saved, raised by Pharaoh's daughter, flees to Midian and encounters God in a burning bush. After ten plagues, the last the death of the Egyptian firstborn, Pharaoh lets the Israelites go, and the sea parts for them.</p><p>At Sinai God pronounces the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-commandments">Ten Commandments</a> and the Covenant Code of ritual and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a>, in Exodus 20:22–23:33, which resembles the Laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> in content and structure. The later chapters give instructions for the Tabernacle and the priesthood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The biblical scholar Carol Meyers calls Exodus arguably the most important book in the Bible, because it presents the defining features of Israel's identity: memories of hardship and escape, a binding covenant with a God who chooses Israel, and guidelines for the life of the community. Its central theme is the Sinaitic covenant, which resembles ancient Middle Eastern treaties but is made between a god and a people rather than between a ruler and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>.</p><p>Most mainstream scholars do not accept the account as historical, and the consensus is that the Pentateuch does not accurately describe the origins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a>, who seem to have formed in the central highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> in the late second millennium BCE. Israel Finkelstein and Neil Asher Silberman argue that archaeology has found no evidence for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-exodus">the Exodus</a> as described. A majority of scholars nonetheless think the story has a historical core, though they disagree about what it was, and the names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>, Aaron and Phinehas seem to be of Egyptian origin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Book of Exodus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;Go Down Moses&quot; is an African American spiritual drawn from the <b>Book of Exodus</b>, chapter 5, verse 1, where God sends Moses to Pharaoh to demand the release of the Israelites from bondage in Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/songs/go-down-moses">Go Down Moses</a></li><li><blockquote>The Song of the Sea, also known by its opening words as Az Yashir Moshe and by a later line as Mi Chamocha, is a poem in the <b>Book of Exodus</b> (15:1–18).</blockquote><a href="/songs/song-of-the-sea">Song of the Sea</a></li></ul></section>
